
<p>Steven Nelson, writing for The New York Post:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>President Trump claimed Monday that Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang could
have become a leader in China’s artificial intelligence industry
if he hadn’t been reinstated to the US firm — telling CNBC in a
phone interview that the 63-year-old has “got a little blood
there” despite being born in Taiwan.</p>

<p>“If we didn’t do this, I could see China coming in and saying to
Jensen, ‘Hey, how would you like to come to China?’ — He’s got
a little [Chinese] blood in him. He’s got a little blood there — ‘How would you like to come to China and head up Huawei?’”
Trump told CNBC in a phone interview, during which he called
Huang, 63, a “national treasure” despite his affair with an
alleged Chinese spy.</p>

<p>“If Jensen was angry enough, if he was treated badly, maybe he
could do that.”</p>
</blockquote>

<p>This was during Trump’s <a href="https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/23/trump-nvidia-jensen-huang.html">50-minute phoner on CNBC</a>. <a href="https://daringfireball.net/linked/2026/11/23/nvidia-board-reinstates-jensen-huang">As I noted</a> yesterday, on his Truth Social blog, <a href="https://truthsocial.com/@realDonaldTrump/posts/117666141823845971">Trump wrote</a>:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>On a personal note, I have gotten to know Jensen, and he is a
Great Patriot and National Treasure.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>Think about how perverse Trump’s worldview is that he simultaneously declares that Jensen Huang is a “Great Patriot and National Treasure” <em>and</em> that Huang would be justified in moving to China to help the United States’ geopolitical archrival in the AI arms race “if he was treated badly”.</p>

